{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-31968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-31968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-31968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-31968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-31968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$W$ and $Y$ only"] ["text" "$W$ and $Z$ only"] ["text" "$W$ only"] ["text" "$Y$ and $Z$ only"] ["text" "$Z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-31968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$W$ and $Z$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $S$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$S$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$S$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $Q$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $W$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $E$ are elementary matrices." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$E$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $E$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $W$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$W$ and $E$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-42751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Q$ and $E$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$  and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$  and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A$  only"] ["text" "$B$  only"] ["text" "$C$  only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$  only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $B$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q