uestion-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 24 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 24 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-130823" "field" n