7a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec0c8d7cf541dee28fbc27596ba03918f83d579da98dbf14b6d1961fcff6f555"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "q-73651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05dcec5af3e14739ec0543887a72108ffce53d494ac321cf42506789a11b244"} {"question" "e-4716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4716.json" "file_sha256" "8356129917df146c7ed724836e5f205528894569cb8c6b3170316950b41cdee3"} {"question" "e-4716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4716.json" "file_sha256" "8356129917df146c7ed724836e5f205528894569cb8c6b3170316950b41cdee3"} {"question" "e-4718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4718.json" "file_sha256" "674290b6d0568e3c76b9ab6d44dfd418b2aa11f4301dd509fcd5bf1949396de8"} {"question" "e-4718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4718.json" "file_sha256" "674290b6d0568e3c76b9ab6d44dfd418b2aa11f4301dd509fcd5bf1949396de8"} {"question" "e-4719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4719.json" "file_sha256" "c08555035fdef4c2faa4463ee2a997222a0f3fc4b50d1ceca156a59351df21fc"} {"question" "e-4719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/example-4719.json" "file_sha256" "c08555035fdef4c2faa4463ee2a997222a0f3fc4b50d1ceca156a59351df21fc"}] "usage" {"e-4718" [] "e-4719" [] "e-4716" []} "basis" 1863 "retractions" [[17592186114989 :question/problem "Is\n$E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nan elementary matrix?"] [17592186114989 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114990 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114990 :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186114991 :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$, where $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114991 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}$. We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E_{2} & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
